    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 092337\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n437 PM PDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  The warming trend peaks on Thursday when the Extreme Heat\n   Warning goes into effect. Temperatures cool into and through \n   the weekend.\n\n*  Spotty storm activity will be possible in the Sierra Nevada and \n   higher elevations of eastern San Bernardino today. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the\n   region Friday through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Tuesday\n\nA strong high pressure continues to build into the region leading\nto mostly clear skies and warming temperatures. Afternoon highs \nlook to peak around 5 degrees above average with most of the \ndesert valleys ranging from the upper 90s to around 105 degrees. \nAreas to the south of Lake Havasu and around Death Valley will \nexceed 110. The warmth will also be felt with well above average \novernight lows, around 10 degrees average. This warm overnight \ncombined with even stronger warming for Thursday afternoon \nincreases overall HeatRisk. As such, an Extreme Heat Warning has \nbeen issued for Thursday and covers Death Valley, the Las Vegas \nValley, Pahrump Valley, Moapa Valley, Colorado River Valley, \nsouthern Mohave County, and southeastern San Bernardino County. \nThe upper level pattern begins to push a trough into the area from\nthe Northwest, pushing the high pressure east, and allowing for a\ncooling trend starting Friday. \n\nIn spite of the strong high pressure, there has been some weak \nconvection in the southern portions of the CWA with the potential \nfor some spotty activity in the Sierra Nevada near Bishop. Radar \nreturns have looked fairly week, and local gauges picking up very \nlittle, so these showers look to not be particularly impactful. \nAdditional chances will arrive to these areas tomorrow afternoon, \nbut again don't look to be impactful.\n\nThe late week pattern change will increase the pressure gradient \nfrom the high pressure to the east to the building trough to the \nnorthwest and result in increasing winds. Friday afternoon will \noffer breezy to gusty winds that look to ease slightly overnight\nbut build further in into Saturday with widespread gusts above \n30 mph possible and above 40 mph possible in higher terrain and \nfavored gaps and passes. Winds look to reduce for Sunday but \nremain breezy. Longer term models are starting to favor breezy \nwinds peaking around 25 mph each afternoon into the next work \nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill generally follow typical daily directional patterns through \nthe forecast period, with light northerly to northeasterly winds \nat issuance expected to veer to the southwest by late evening. \nWind speeds will remain under 10KT until mid- to late afternoon \nThursday, when a southwesterly shift with speeds increasing to \naround 10-15KT is expected. VFR conditions will continue, with \npassing bands of mid- level and high clouds at or above 15kft. \nTemperatures will drop below 100 degrees after 02Z this evening, \nand will exceed 100 degrees again on Thursday between 19Z-03Z.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds will generally\nfollow typical daily directional patterns over the next 24 hours,\nwith speeds around 5-10KT. Elevated northerly down-valley winds \nin the vicinity of KIFP will diminish early in the forecast \nperiod, with another round of these winds expected mid-morning \nthrough early afternoon Thursday. Additionally, across the western\nMojave Desert/KDAG, winds will largely maintain a westerly wind \ndirection with speeds around 8-12KT. VFR conditions will prevail, \nwith passing bands of clouds with bases at or above 12kft. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
